<!--
  BlockML VS Code — documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.vscode.documentation.Introduction
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.vscode.documentation"
  xmlns:vscode="org.blockml.bml.vscode">

  <name>Introduction</name>
  <type>doc:Introduction</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A short introduction to the BlockML VS Code library</is>

  <documentation>
    org.blockml.bml.vscode is the BlockML library that describes the VS Code / Cursor extension
    for authoring .bml files. The library is the single source of truth for extension identity,
    contributions (languages, grammars, icons, settings, LSP, navigation), and curated
    documentation of the V1 / V2 roadmap.

    The intended workflow: humans and LLMs edit BML under blocks/org/blockml/bml/vscode/; a
    generator (or LLM coding pass) materializes the runnable extension (package.json, TextMate
    grammar, icons, TypeScript client). Regenerating from BML updates the plugin without
    drifting from the model.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see type="doc:PluginArchitecture" relation="ReadNext">
      Next — Extension, contributions, and packages.
    </see>
    <see type="vscode:Extension" relation="Extends">
      Root descriptor type for a VS Code extension in BML.
    </see>
    <see type="vscode:BlockMlExtension" relation="SeeAlso">
      Concrete BlockML extension catalogue.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
